# Training config for the DM_mBlipEnv many-body descriptor.
#
# DM_mBlipEnv mixes TWO different basis families:
#   * the atomic DENSITY rho is built from a Gaussian radial basis -
#     Gaussian-type orbitals  exp(-eta*(r-r_s)^2)  summed over neighbours;
#   * the EMBEDDING function F(rho) is built from blip (cubic B-spline)
#     basis functions in rho-space, each multiplied by an inverse-cutoff
#     envelope (Cut_SinInv) of width RCUTENV.
#
# The blip embedding is a flexible spline basis: given enough blips it can
# represent essentially ANY EAM-style embedding function F(rho). The
# envelope enforces the physical EAM boundary condition  F(rho) -> 0  as
# rho -> 0  (an atom with no neighbours contributes no embedding energy)
# by smoothly ramping F down over the low-density region rho < RCUTENV.

DBFILE   tdata.db          # 50 thermal MD snapshots of bcc Ta
                           # (54-atom 3x3x3 supercells, a0 ~ 3.32 Angstrom)

INITMB   true              # use a many-body descriptor
MODEL    M_BLR BF_Linear   # linear Bayesian regression

# Per-species density weight. WATOM scales every neighbour's contribution
# to rho; ATOM lists the elements and WATOM the matching weights.
# The raw radial basis below gives a median density ~3.24 for this
# dataset; WATOM = 1/3.24 rescales it so the per-atom density is ~1.
ATOM     Ta
WATOM    0.309

# TYPEMB params: descriptor, L, size(CGRIDMB), size(SGRIDMB),
#                size(CEMBFUNC), size(SEMBFUNC), element pair
#
#   L = 0  -> only the s (l=0) orbital is used.
#     RESTRICTION on L: DM_mBlipEnv is rotationally invariant ONLY for
#     L = 0. Its embedding function is not F_SQ (rho^2), so for L > 0 the
#     embedding is applied to individual orbital densities before the
#     invariance-restoring quadratic sum and the descriptor is NOT
#     rotationally invariant (a runtime WARNING is emitted). Lmax is also
#     hard-capped at 6. Keep L = 0 here; use the DM_PS_* descriptors if a
#     rotationally invariant L > 0 many-body descriptor is required.
#
#   CGRIDMB / SGRIDMB must have size 1: the dimension-expanding blip
#     embedding requires a single radial basis function. The descriptor
#     output dimension is set by CEMBFUNC / SEMBFUNC instead (here 5).
TYPEMB   DM_mBlipEnv 0 1 1 5 5 Ta Ta
RCTYPEMB Cut_Cos           # real-space cutoff applied to the radial basis
RCUTMB   5.0               # real-space cutoff radius (Angstrom)

# --- radial Gaussian basis (defines the DENSITY rho) -------------------
CGRIDMB  2.88              # Gaussian centre r_s (Angstrom): bcc-Ta 1st shell
SGRIDMB  8.0               # Gaussian inverse width eta (Angstrom^-2); eta > 0

# --- blip embedding basis (defines the EMBEDDING F(rho)) ---------------
# With the WATOM above, the per-atom density of this dataset lies in
# rho ~ [0.89, 1.10] (median ~1.00). The 5 blips are deliberately WIDE
# (small SEMBFUNC): each has a rho-support of width 4/eta = 2.0, so every
# blip overlaps the data band - its weight is constrained by the fit -
# AND the lower blips reach all the way down to rho = 0. The fitted
# embedding F(rho) is therefore finite and non-zero for every rho > 0;
# there is no flat dead zone where the blip basis vanishes.
CEMBFUNC 0.5 0.75 1.0 1.25 1.5   # blip centres in rho-space (5)
SEMBFUNC 2.0 2.0 2.0 2.0 2.0     # blip inverse widths in rho-space (5)

RCUTENV  0.6               # envelope width in rho-space (Cut_SinInv): F is
                           # ramped 0 -> 1 over rho in [0, RCUTENV] and
                           # saturated to 1 above. The wide blips give a
                           # NON-zero embedding at rho = 0; the envelope is
                           # what cancels it, smoothly pulling F -> 0 as
                           # rho -> 0. It is already saturated to 1 across
                           # this dataset's rho band (~0.9-1.1), so the fit
                           # where training data is present is undistorted.

LAMBDA   1e-7              # regulariser
OALGO    1                 # solver
